#{[#edb/ref 17592186050711 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065258} :activity/id #uuid "3159ca73-1d98-5540-ba3d-ce5c4dd41cdb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065138 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064104 :question/id #uuid "fe999c48-1afd-5949-b4f9-533c09511d95" :question/math-academy-id "e-5295" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3x^2+1$ where the tangent line has a slope of $-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3x^2+1.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f'(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =6x+0 \\\\  & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f'(x) = -1$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-1 \\\\ 6x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where $x=-\\dfrac 1 6.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Curve With a Certain Slope"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065368 :tutorial/content "Remember that the derivative of a function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) represents the slope of the tangent line to the curve ![](images/e2/e2ed4a9881134f510823ba710f835acf5720b866f0df86fa6cd24448524431d6.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the curve ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg), shown below. If we want to compute the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point ![](images/3a/3a613cb22e7bfd4038442b830babc21756061f5b18d1b1969451c8403d2894a3.svg) then we compute ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) and evaluate ![](images/91/910d575ac485dde82a562406c34e5e80677b10d5fb0e38b3370558d22badc908.svg)\n\n![](images/63/63f675532755376a733f2f0cad2b40e55f19b3278c1bcc5e8cb355fc6bdac277.png)\n\nIf ![](images/01/01ced1892dd48d3cbba7806664f3a1d763ef692dd88e5bf031d84db220141410.svg) then ![](images/a9/a9460f8b0abe06f33ff168a2a1887572f1c282de78c982c3af65c1c0670b9818.svg) by the power rule. Substituting ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) into ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) gives\n![](images/e0/e046ab7a11915ca797bd74e8e304484fe46ecaf4d2f19429409a4a8c6a3b1456.svg)\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) at the point ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065259}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064342 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063980 :question/id #uuid "9c9b5972-f775-5cbe-8e66-2a0309f24f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-142" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot x-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)-(2{)}^{-2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\dfrac{15}{4}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve at a Given Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064343 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063983 :question/id #uuid "3138f267-3903-510c-86e8-5597d3689262" :question/math-academy-id "e-143" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "This time, we'll use $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {x}^{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{-1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =4(1{)}^{3}+2(1{)}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve With Fractional Powers at a Given Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065138}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064423}] [#edb/ref 17592186050372 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069834} :activity/id #uuid "37643965-adaf-5a8f-893a-965115fd6336" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069995 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + {\\color{blue}{a}}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNot all quadratic expressions are perfect squares. However, every quadratic expression can be expressed as a perfect square *plus a constant*. In this lesson, we'll learn how to write quadratic expressions in this form.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 6x + 3^2\\right) + 4\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully written the original expression as a perfect square $(x + {\\color{blue}3})^2$ plus a constant $(4).$\n\nWriting a quadratic expression as a perfect square plus a constant is called **completing the square.** Completing the square is a valuable technique for solving many problems.  \n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068672 :question/id #uuid "2acac780-2b17-5489-accc-2a3a090784e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9927" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 + 14x + 49) - 8$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+14x+49 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+14x+49)-8 & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2}-8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068677 :question/id #uuid "b107ed10-4501-50ad-8ad7-231fd933bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9997" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 - 12x + 36) -5$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+36 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-12x+36)-5 & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2}-5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069946 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've completed the square on quadratic expressions whose perfect square is already isolated. \n\nIn general, isolating the perfect square in a quadratic expression requires a trick. We'll now learn how this works.\n\nAs an example, let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2+10x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term ($10$) as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear coefficient is now decomposed into two factors, $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}.$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and then *subtract* the square of the second factor ${\\color{blue}{5}},$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{15}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{5}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the last two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can easily check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+15 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Completing the Square"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/math-academy-id "e-10008" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  + 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :question/id #uuid "06ed73b3-1442-5b21-a317-961d0395f7da" :question/math-academy-id "e-10009" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  +4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}}] :activity/title "Completing the Square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068977}] [#edb/ref 17592186050860 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093313} :activity/id #uuid "519d74ef-0d20-51ee-956a-2d9ac3c4b6ff" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093352 :tutorial/content "Suppose we would like to find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t}, \\quad y=2\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/83/836757696669a0852812a63c8cafcf9ac3bddd73558f7b3e67c8427ec39c008a.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length $L$ of a curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nis given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe intuition behind this formula is given at the end of this tutorial.\n\nAccording to the formula, the length of the blue arc is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}t))}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}t+{cos}^{2}t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092375 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092081 :question/id #uuid "1957007f-eabd-5d40-8f59-ff635ea1d539" :question/math-academy-id "e-602" :question/problem "![](images/83/834eff403ac2a6f696e104ec914cac1b951f8a6d989e11c0e9f2576ac9e7f0d8.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})={t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{({t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092113 :question/id #uuid "5bae6269-2112-56ea-b123-bee58701cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-7130" :question/problem "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](images/48/48de26c11ae08afb45ec19ee56e770f7418f9be2afb8ee7dd46a0d1136cb125b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092377 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092082 :question/id #uuid "86835544-a7f1-5be2-99c1-14e39a14f3fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-603" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}) , \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right].\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15d185178bf8882f837c19361d630c0e537e145ca2337cad4655677255d0348a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093353 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our first example, which was to find the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t}, \\quad y=2\\sin{t}, \\quad t\\in\\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$\n\n![](images/83/836757696669a0852812a63c8cafcf9ac3bddd73558f7b3e67c8427ec39c008a.png)\n\nFirst, we divide the interval $\\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right]$ into small subintervals of equal length. Consider a part of the blue arc that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of that part be $\\Delta{s}$.\n\n![](images/35/3502e1055e3c22ef338dcfa015a4d603f808e9db5d3afea75391edddb440801a.png)\n\nWe can now denote the horizontal displacement as $\\Delta{x}$ and the vertical shift as $\\Delta{y}.$ \n\n![](images/ed/ed47d9cb8d8bce8514bc876870a5f3e175a87d2e59817ddbb3e10fd8094b24d3.png)\n\nTo approximate the length $\\Delta{s}$ we apply the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δs & \\approx \\sqrt{{(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo approximate the total length $L,$ we need to sum up all of the little $\\Delta s$ terms, so\n\n$$\nL \\approx \\sum \\Delta s = \\sum \\sqrt{\\left( \\Delta{x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\Delta{y} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe then take the limit as $\\Delta x, \\Delta y \\rightarrow 0.$ As we do this, the sum becomes a definite integral, and our approximation approaches the exact value of $L$. So, using the chain rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int \\sqrt{{(\\text{d}x)}^{2}+{(\\text{d}y)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\text{d}t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\text{d}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\text{d}t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\text{d}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "The Intuition Behind the Main Formula"}}] :activity/title "The Arc Length of a Parametric Curve" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092468}] [#edb/ref 17592186050158 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125265} :activity/id #uuid "27f87e6f-03c7-52b0-bd22-1d95ebba4299" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125723 :tutorial/content "Just as the derivative of a function represents the slope of the tangent line to the function, the partial derivative of a function has a geometric interpretation as well.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the surface $z = f (x, y)= 18 - x^2 - y^2$ and the plane $x=3.$ The intersection of the surface and the plane forms a downward parabolic curve, as shown below. A point $P(3,2,5)$ is plotted and a tangent line is drawn through that point.\n\n![](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\n   \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent, we can compute the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ and evaluate it at $P(3,2,5)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(18-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2y \\\\ {\\frac{∂f}{∂y}|}_{P} & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To verify that this result is correct, we can find the equation of the downward parabolic curve and then use single-variable calculus to compute the slope of the tangent line.\n\nTo find the equation of the downward parabolic curve, we can substitute the equation of the plane ($x=3$) into the equation of the surface ($f (x, y)= 18 - x^2 - y^2$). Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,y) & =18-{3}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\  & =9-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the downward parabolic curve is $z=g(y) = 9-y^2.$ In the $yz$-plane, this curve has the following graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent to $g(y)$ at $P,$ we can use single-variable calculus. We differentiate $g(y)$ and evaluate it at $P(3,2,5).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(y) & =-2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(2) & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125465}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124241 :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125267}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124243 :question/id #uuid "75c0deaf-38a7-5a81-afac-cae2978d52b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3070" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125808 :tutorial/content "To recap, when we calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate $f$ with respect to $y$ and treat $x$ as a constant. This is the same as finding the intersection of $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $x = x_0$ and calculating the slope of the resulting curve.\n\nSo, we have the following result:\n\n*Suppose that a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a plane $x = x_0$ intersect on a curve $C,$ and suppose that $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ lies on $C.$ Then $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ evaluated at $P$ gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$*\n\nWe have a similar result for the intersection of a surface and a plane $y=y_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose that a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a plane $y = y_0$ intersect on a curve $C,$ and suppose that $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ lies on $C.$ Then $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ evaluated at $P$ gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$*" :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of the Partial Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125266}}] :activity/title "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124741}]}
